# Fingerprints gitleaks should not re-report. Format: <commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>
#
# The secret-detection job in .github/workflows/security.yml scans the FULL git
# history (`gitleaks git`), which is the point — a secret committed and later
# deleted is still exposed to anyone who clones. That also means every finding
# ever recorded stays findable, so genuinely-benign historical matches have to be
# retired by fingerprint rather than by editing the past.
#
# Everything below was reviewed on 2026-08-16 against the first successful scan
# of this repository. Nothing here is a live credential. Each pair of entries is
# the same change recorded twice, under both the GitLab and the GitHub author —
# see CLAUDE.md on the commit-authorship rewrite.
#
# Add an entry ONLY after confirming the match is not a real secret. For new code
# prefer an inline `# gitleaks:allow` comment, which reviewers see in the diff —
# it also survives history rewrites, and a fingerprint does not. The commit in
# each line below is part of the key, so rebasing or re-authoring a commit that
# an entry names silently un-ignores it. CLAUDE.md's rule about rewriting pulled
# commits to benson-priori makes that churn routine, so if this file starts
# reporting again after a rewrite, re-run the scan and refresh the fingerprints
# rather than assuming a new secret appeared:
#
#   gitleaks git . --redact --report-format json --report-path leaks.json

